;;; An `(absent …)` over a string always holds — M1e2 **W18**, register row
;;; `W18/str-in-absent` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), found by S1e2.2.18
;;; (2026-11-04) probing the case's mechanism.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two `holds` facts, the atom `a` and the string `"b"`, which the loader
;;; holds as the symbol `b`. `z` concludes `(hit z)` while `a` holds and
;;; `"b"` does not.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A string in a rule means what it means in a fact, so `(absent (holds
;;; "b"))` asks after the fact `(holds b)`, which the program states: `z`
;;; never fires. The blind enumerator proposes `(hit a)` and `(hit b)`, so R
;;; is four states and none holds `(hit z)`. It is the answer of this file
;;; with `"b"` written `b` in its rule.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `z` fires: the rule compiler does not lower a string, so the pattern
;;; under `absent` is an opaque slot that matches nothing, and its `absent`
;;; always holds. A conclusion the program refutes is in every state: `ein
;;; solve FILE --minimal` at `ff023413` prints root's state as
;;; `(model (hit z))`, in a release build and a debug one.

(relation holds Node) (relation hit Node)
(holds a) (holds "b")
(rule z () :match (and (holds a) (absent (holds "b"))) :assert (hit z))
